window.DEVLOG_INDEX = [{"t": "Porting NVIDIA DLSS from Unreal 5.8 to 5.9 in 75 minutes", "s": "NVIDIA's DLSS 4.5 plugin stops at Unreal 5.8. Six compiles, three editor launches and one crash later it runs on our 5.9 engine, without Frame Generation.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0605-dlss-on-unreal-5-9.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794463500.0, "i": "media/dlss-six-compiles.thumb.jpg", "x": "I asked for this one to be written up \"even if it doesn't work\". It mostly worked. Why bother. The NVIDIA RTX Award needs an entry that uses DLSS 4.5, an NvRTX feature or NVIDIA ACE. DLSS is the only realistic one for an RTX 2060. NVIDIA's plugin (v8.9.0) supports Unreal 5.5 to 5.8, and the jam engine is a 5.9 source build. The plugin ships with full source, so a port was possible. Claude and I did it on Wednesday 11 November, before the jam, as tooling. It's declared on the Made before the jam page. Six compiles The download is 1.1 GB with 11 plugins. We took five of them (20 C++ modules) into an empty test project, so the real project was never at risk. Six compile attempts. The first two failures were ours, the next three were one Unreal 5.9 change, and the sixth passed. Attempt 1: a quoting mistake in the build command. Ours, not NVIDIA's. Attempt 2: Windows' 260-character path limit. The test folder's path was long enough that build files went past it. Still a thing in 2026. Attempt 3: two minutes in, most of it compiled. Every failure was the same thing: Unreal 5.9 checks log-message format strings at compile time, and the 5.8 code passes 64-bit numbers and enum values to %d and %u . Attempts 4 and 5: more of the same, patched by hand each time. Attempt 6: \"Result: Succeeded\" in 2 minutes 16 seconds. The fix we should have looked for first Three compiles of hand patches, then the switch Unreal 5.9 provides for exactly this. After patching 12 log messages one at a time, "}, {"t": "Pre-production: everything that existed before the clock", "s": "Two evenings of setup before the jam: a crew of Claude Code sessions, a smoke test that found 11 problems, the rules, the theme reveal and a Q&A. No game.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0555-pre-production.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 1", "o": 1794462900.0, "i": "media/preprod-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "I'm Daniel Johnson, and for the next 93 hours I'm making a game called Reel Time with a crew of Claude Code sessions. This post is the one-stop record of what existed before the clock started at 05:00 on Thursday 12 November. The short version: tooling, plans and one engine plugin. No game. The jam's rules allow exactly that (§7(h)): \"Entrants may prepare AI tools, agent configurations, Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\") settings, prompt libraries, and scripts before the Contest Period. [...] All other game content, including gameplay, levels, and art assets (other than noted sourced assets), must be created during the Contest Period.\" Two evenings of setup, then the clock. The game project's first commit is at 05:27 on Thursday, 27 minutes into the jam. The crew Each Claude Code session has one job, its own folders and an inbox folder. I talk to two of them: the Director, who turns what I say into tasks, and the Devlog, who writes this site. Only the Engine session touches the Unreal Editor. Everyone else hands it a \"kit\", a script that builds their assets and can be rerun. Who talks to whom. Every arrow is a file dropped in an inbox folder. A task's journey, from a request to something a player can see. In numbers: 12 role files, 28 coordination scripts, 7 hooks, 3 subagents and 2 skills. By day the Director and Devlog run on Claude Fable 5.2 and most builders on Opus 5.5. Anything done on a smaller model has to be reviewed at Opus level before it counts as done. The smoke test"}];
